{"tool_name":"predictnls","contents":["biocontainers","bioschemas","debian","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"predictnls","identifiers":["biotools:predictnls","doi:10.1093/embo-reports/kvd092"],"license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Prediction and analysis of nuclear localization signals.","home_url":"https://www.rostlab.org/owiki/index.php/PredictNLS","keywords":["Animals","Cell Nucleus","Computer Simulation","Cytoplasm","DNA","DNA-Binding Proteins","Databases, Factual","Genome","Humans","Models, Biological","Mutagenesis","Nuclear Localization Signals","Protein Transport","RNA","Protein sites, features and motifs","Sequence analysis"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":0},"biotools":{"id":"predictnls","home":"https://www.rostlab.org/owiki/index.php/PredictNLS","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Prediction and analysis of nuclear localization signals.","addition_date":"2015-12-02T17:59:47Z","last_update_date":"2020-03-27T09:24:25Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application"],"version":["1.0.18"],"collections":["PredictProtein","Rostlab tools"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:predictnls","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"help@predictprotein.org","name":"Support","typeRole":["Support"]},{"email":"help@predictprotein.org","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"Columbia University","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]},{"name":"Murat Cokol","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"name":"RostLab","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]},{"name":"Burkhard Rost","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Rajesh Nair","typeRole":["Contributor"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://rostlab.org/owiki/index.php/PredictNLS"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["Yohan"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Protein sequence record","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2886"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA-like (text)","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2200"}]}],"note":"Prediction of nuclear localization signals (NLS)","operation":[{"term":"Protein feature detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3092"},{"term":"Protein subcellular localisation prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2489"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Nucleic acid features","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1276"},"format":[{"term":"Protein report format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2062"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"PredictNLS","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"RostLab","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/embo-reports/kvd092","metadata":{"abstract":"A variety of nuclear localization signals (NLSs) are experimentally known although only one motif was available for database searches through PROSITE. We initially collected a set of 91 experimentally verified NLSs from the literature. Through iterated 'in silica mutagenesis' we then extended the set to 214 potential NLSs. This final set matched in 43% of all known nuclear proteins and in no known non-nuclear protein. We estimated that >17% of all eukaryotic proteins may be imported into the nucleus. Finally, we found an overlap between the NLS and DNA-binding region for 90% of the proteins for which both the NLS and DNA-binding regions were known. Thus, evolution seemed to have used part of the existing DNA-binding mechanism when compartmentalizing DNA-binding proteins into the nucleus. However, only 56 of our 214 NLS motifs overlapped with DNA-binding regions. These 56 NLSs enabled a de novo prediction of partial DNA-binding regions for \u223c800 proteins in human, fly, worm and yeast. \u00a9 2000 European Molecular Biology Organization.","authors":[{"name":"Cokol M."},{"name":"Nair R."},{"name":"Rost B."}],"citationCount":528,"date":"2000-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"EMBO Reports","title":"Finding nuclear localization signals"},"pmcid":"PMC1083765","pmid":"11258480","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Protein sites, features and motifs","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3510"}